WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.580
- Republican Congressman
Mo Brooks hoists himself

00:00:02.580 --> 00:00:05.140
on his own petard by screwing himself

00:00:05.140 --> 00:00:07.180
out of getting a defense
from the government.

00:00:07.180 --> 00:00:09.490
You might recall that Eric
Swalwell filed a lawsuit

00:00:09.490 --> 00:00:11.570
against some of the people
he believes are responsible

00:00:11.570 --> 00:00:13.400
for the January 6th insurrection.

00:00:13.400 --> 00:00:15.900
Congressman Mo Brooks tried
to get out of the suit

00:00:15.900 --> 00:00:17.390
by invoking the Westfall Act,

00:00:17.390 --> 00:00:19.500
which basically says you can't
sue a government official

00:00:19.500 --> 00:00:21.340
for acts that occurred
during the course and scope

00:00:21.340 --> 00:00:22.910
of their governmental duties.

00:00:22.910 --> 00:00:24.970
And if the Westfall Act
applies, this is complicated,

00:00:24.970 --> 00:00:27.030
but effectively, the
official just gets dismissed

00:00:27.030 --> 00:00:27.863
from the suit.

00:00:27.863 --> 00:00:29.950
Now, Mo Brooks claimed that
when he was making his speech

00:00:29.950 --> 00:00:32.410
on January 6th, this was
in the course and scope

00:00:32.410 --> 00:00:33.930
of his duties as a Congressman.

00:00:33.930 --> 00:00:36.510
But in trying to defeat the lawsuit itself

00:00:36.510 --> 00:00:38.320
and invoke this privilege, he said

00:00:38.320 --> 00:00:40.960
that he was basically making
political campaign speeches,

00:00:40.960 --> 00:00:42.740
and there are all kinds of regulations

00:00:42.740 --> 00:00:43.990
that say a sitting congressperson

00:00:43.990 --> 00:00:46.410
cannot make campaign speeches
in the course and scope

00:00:46.410 --> 00:00:48.830
of their duties as an elected official.

00:00:48.830 --> 00:00:51.050
So by Mo Brooks's own sworn testimony,

00:00:51.050 --> 00:00:52.360
the Westfall Act doesn't apply,

00:00:52.360 --> 00:00:54.530
and the DOJ is not going to defend him.

00:00:54.530 --> 00:00:57.080
So we will definitely be
seeing Mo Brooks in court.

